sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Although planetary motion looks regular on human timescales, sensitive dependence on initial conditions should ultimately occur in the solar system, producing significant randomness over a few million years that may explain some present-day features of the solar system.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Solar system motion should show sensitive dependence over millions of years";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “Chaos Theory and Randomness from Initial Conditions” (from p. 304), p. 314. Scope: orbital dynamics of planets and other bodies in the solar system"
}
